        "chapterId": 246,
        "headingId": 160,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>9751/1 Langerhans cell histiocytosis NOS</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-10T11:39:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 361,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 161,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 55721,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 246,
        "headingId": 161,
        "headingText": "<p>2B31.2Y Other specified Langerhans cell histiocytosis</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-16T14:54:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 362,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 162,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 55722,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 246,
        "headingId": 162,
        "headingText": "<p><em>Acceptable:</em> histiocytosis&nbsp;X; eosinophilic granuloma.</p>\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> Langerhans cell granulomatosis.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-05-02T11:38:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 363,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 163,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 55723,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 246,
        "headingId": 163,
        "headingText": "<p>Langerhans cell histiocytosis, disseminated (9751/3)</p>",
        "timestamp": "2021-11-22T17:07:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 364,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 164,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 55724,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 246,
        "headingId": 164,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>LCH can involve a single or multiple sites, most commonly bone and skin. Gastrointestinal and liver involvement is common in paediatric patients with multisystem LCH and uncommon in adults.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-10T11:39:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 365,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 165,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 55725,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 246,
        "headingId": 165,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Children with multisystem LCH are usually aged &lt;&nbsp;2&nbsp;years, and they present with failure to thrive, diarrhoea, vomiting, protein-losing enteropathy, and hepatomegaly. They have multiple ulcerative lesions throughout the intestines {2178934; 10229501; 21263252}. Adults with GI tract involvement are asymptomatic or present with constipation, anaemia, or dysphagia {21263252}. They often have a solitary polypoid or ulcerative lesion in the large intestine or (less commonly) the stomach and oesophagus {21263252; 25268199}. Liver involvement in adults usually occurs in the setting of multiorgan disease, and it manifests as hepatomegaly and abnormal liver biochemistry (in particular, evidence of cholestasis) {10229501; 21550330}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-10T11:39:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 366,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 166,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 55726,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 246,
        "headingId": 166,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The annual incidence in children is 4.1&ndash;5.4&nbsp;cases per million person-years, and the M:F&nbsp;ratio is 1.2:1 to 2.2:1 {19060008; 18260177; 9561907}. LCH has a wide age range at presentation, but is more common in children.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-10T11:39:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 367,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
